<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gaudiya Vaishnavism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gaudiya Vaishnava, Bengal Vaishnavism</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gaudiya <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishnavism">Vaishnavism</a> is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> devotional movement founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaitanya-mahaprabhu">Chaitanya Mahaprabhu</a> (1486–1534), centred on the worship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> and Radha through ecstatic chanting of their names. Its theology was set out by Chaitanya's followers, especially the brothers Rupa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanatana-goswami">Sanatana Goswami</a> at Vrindavan. It shaped Bengali literature and religion and later spread worldwide through the International Society for Krishna Consciousness.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gaudiya-vaishnavism.jpg" alt="Gaudiya Vaishnavism" width="320" height="284" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chaitanya_dances_with_followers.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
